  "result": "Fixed. The command now prints `27.06`, and all 6 tests pass.\n\n**Root cause:** `to_cents` in `shop/money.py:9` padded the fractional part on the wrong side. It used `frac.rjust(2, \"0\")`, which turned `\"12.5\"` into 12 dollars and 5 cents (1205) instead of 12 dollars and 50 cents (1250). That gave 2 \u00d7 12.05 = 24.10, plus 8.25% tax, which comes to 26.09.\n\n**Fix:** I changed it to `frac.ljust(2, \"0\")`. Now `\"12.5\"` \u2192 1250, so the total is 25.00 + 2.06 tax = 27.06. Other inputs still parse correctly: `\"12.05\"` \u2192 1205, `\"12\"` \u2192 1200, `\"0.1\"` \u2192 10.\n\nNone of the existing tests use a price with only one decimal place, which is why they didn't catch this. I can add a regression test for that if you'd like.",
